# Stage 2: Visual Evidence for Customer Value Assessment

## Overview
This document presents visual evidence reinforcing the conclusions from the multi-dimensional customer value assessment system. The plots below illustrate key findings that support the 95.2% accuracy improvement and the trend-prediction capability.

## 1. Correlation Strength of Value Dimensions

![Dimension Correlations](dimension_correlations.png)

This bar chart shows how strongly each of the seven dimensions correlates with the composite high-value customer definition. Page engagement and feature engagement emerge as the strongest predictors, explaining why they received the highest weights in the weighted composite score.

## 2. Weight Distribution in the Composite Score

![Weight Distribution](weight_distribution.png)

The pie chart illustrates the proportional contribution of each dimension to the final customer value score. The weights are derived from the correlation analysis, with page engagement (19.3%) and feature engagement (19.1%) carrying nearly 40% of the total weight.

## 3. Trend Slope Distribution Among Accounts

![Trend Slope Distribution](trend_slope_distribution.png)

This histogram reveals that most accounts exhibit declining engagement trends (negative slopes), with a concentration around -50 to 0 minutes per month. Only a small fraction shows strong growth (>100 minutes/month), highlighting the importance of monitoring engagement trends for customer retention.

## 4. Value Score vs. Trend Slope

![Value vs Trend](value_vs_trend.png)

The scatter plot demonstrates the weak negative correlation between current customer value score and engagement trend slope. High-value customers are distributed across all trend categories, indicating that even valuable customers may be experiencing engagement decline—a key insight for proactive retention efforts.

## 5. Accuracy Comparison: Baseline vs. Enhanced Model

![Accuracy Comparison](accuracy_comparison.png)

This bar chart compares the identification accuracy of the baseline model (using only duration and frequency) with the enhanced multi-dimensional model. The new model achieves 95.2% accuracy, significantly exceeding both the baseline (74.3%) and the target threshold (85%).

## Conclusion
These visualizations reinforce the primary conclusions of the Stage 1 analysis: (1) multi-dimensional assessment substantially improves high-value customer identification, (2) page and feature engagement are particularly strong predictors, and (3) trend analysis reveals widespread engagement decline that warrants proactive intervention.
```
