'''
# Stage2: Visual Evidence

## 1. Departmental Conversion Trend
![Engineering vs Marketing conversion rate trend](dept_trend.png)

The line chart depicts the monthly application-to-interview conversion rates for Engineering and Marketing departments over the last six months. It clearly shows Engineering’s conversion rate declining from ~35% to ~22% while Marketing remained stable above 45%, reinforcing the primary finding that Engineering has experienced a pronounced drop.

## 2. Engineering Roles Performance
![Conversion rates by Engineering role](engineering_roles_trend.png)

This multi-line plot tracks conversion rates for each major role in Engineering. Frontend and Backend roles exhibit the steepest declines, while Data Engineer roles show more fluctuation but also contribute to the overall drop. The visual confirms that the downturn is not uniform across roles.

## 3. Hiring Manager Impact
![Conversion rates by Hiring Manager](hiring_manager_impact.png)

Bar chart comparing average conversion rates of hiring managers within the Engineering department. A few managers (e.g., Manager ID 4567) have rates below 20%, while others maintain over 35%. This highlights systematic differences in screening effectiveness across hiring managers.

## 4. Interview Load and Conversion Relationship
![Conversions vs Interview Count](scatter_interviews_conv.png)

Scatter plot of conversion rate against the total number of interviews per job. The moderate positive correlation (r≈0.67) indicates that jobs with more interviews tend to have higher conversion rates, suggesting the decline in conversion is not explained by interview volume solely. Further analysis is needed to examine per‑interviewer load effects.

## Conclusion
Together, the four visualizations substantiate the Stage1 findings: a sharp decline in Engineering’s conversion rate, with specific roles driving the decline and notable variation among hiring managers. The scatter plot adds nuance that interview load alone does not explain the drop, pointing instead to screening processes or other factors.
''')
